window.QUIZ2_BANK.push(...[{"id":201,"targetId":201,"type":"tc","text":"The board could forgive an honest accounting error, but to {{1}} the deliberate concealment of losses would undermine its claim to ethical leadership.","choices":[["condone","expose","forestall","document","rectify"]],"answers":[["condone"]],"explanation":"The contrast is between forgiving a mistake and tolerating intentional misconduct. Condone means overlook or accept wrongdoing; the other choices would reveal, prevent, record, or correct it.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":5,"initial":"c"},{"id":202,"targetId":202,"type":"tc","text":"The archive’s open catalog was {{1}} to independent research: by removing the need for personal introductions, it reduced an institutional {{2}} that had excluded outsiders.","choices":[["conducive","inimical","irrelevant"],["impediment","incentive","advantage"]],"answers":[["conducive"],["impediment"]],"explanation":"The catalog helps research, so it is conducive to it. A requirement that excluded outsiders was an impediment, or obstacle, rather than a benefit.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"impediment","meaning":"an obstacle"}],"section":5,"initial":"c"},{"id":203,"targetId":203,"type":"se","text":"What began as a small warehouse blaze became a {{1}} when wind carried burning debris across the densely built district.","choices":[["conflagration","inferno","respite","deluge","stalemate","procession"]],"answers":[["conflagration","inferno"]],"explanation":"The spreading destructive fire supports both conflagration and inferno. A deluge is a flood; the remaining options describe a pause, deadlock, or moving group.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"inferno","meaning":"an intense destructive fire"}],"section":5,"initial":"c"},{"id":204,"targetId":204,"type":"tc","text":"The species’ survival in soil containing virtually no nitrogen appeared to {{1}} the prevailing theory that abundant nitrogen was indispensable.","choices":[["confound","corroborate","illustrate","popularize","simplify"]],"answers":[["confound"]],"explanation":"Survival under supposedly impossible conditions defeats the theory’s expectations. Confound captures that contradiction; corroborate and illustrate would support the theory.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":5,"initial":"c"},{"id":205,"targetId":205,"type":"tc","text":"Although the two scholars disagreed on almost every conclusion, their shared delight in rigorous debate made them {{1}} colleagues; disagreement never became personal {{2}}.","choices":[["congenial","interchangeable","furtive"],["enmity","affinity","deference"]],"answers":[["congenial"],["enmity"]],"explanation":"Their enjoyment of debate makes them compatible, or congenial. The second blank denies that intellectual differences became personal hostility, or enmity.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[],"section":5,"initial":"c"},{"id":206,"targetId":206,"type":"tc","text":"Until a dated manuscript is discovered, any account of who first formulated the theorem must remain {{1}}, however confidently it is repeated.","choices":[["conjectural","definitive","empirical","exhaustive","irrefutable"]],"answers":[["conjectural"]],"explanation":"The missing evidence leaves the account based on conjecture. Repetition and confidence cannot make an unsupported origin story definitive or empirical.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":5,"initial":"c"},{"id":207,"targetId":207,"type":"tc","text":"The collector deferred to a {{1}} when buying the vase, but the supposed expert’s verdict rested on {{2}} rather than analysis; a plausible story received {{3}} that the physical evidence did not justify.","choices":[["connoisseur","novice","benefactor"],["guesswork","measurement","experimentation"],["credence","censure","resistance"]],"answers":[["connoisseur"],["guesswork"],["credence"]],"explanation":"A connoisseur judges artistic quality. The contrast with analysis calls for guesswork, and the plausible story was believed, receiving credence, without sufficient evidence.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"guesswork","meaning":"judgment made without adequate evidence"},{"word":"credence","meaning":"belief or acceptance as true"}],"section":5,"initial":"c"},{"id":208,"targetId":208,"type":"se","text":"After each faction accepted the revised terms, the chair could finally announce a {{1}} on a proposal that had divided the council for months.","choices":[["consensus","accord","reprimand","quandary","secession","rebuke"]],"answers":[["consensus","accord"]],"explanation":"All factions accepting the terms establishes general agreement: consensus or accord. The other nouns indicate criticism, uncertainty, or withdrawal.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"accord","meaning":"agreement between parties"}],"section":5,"initial":"c"},{"id":209,"targetId":209,"type":"tc","text":"The architects wanted the extension to be {{1}}, not hidden, yet its materials were chosen to {{2}} rather than clash with those of the original museum.","choices":[["conspicuous","imperceptible","subterranean"],["complement","supplant","discredit"]],"answers":[["conspicuous"],["complement"]],"explanation":"The extension should be noticeable, hence conspicuous. Its compatible materials complement the old building; visibility need not mean aesthetic conflict.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"complement","meaning":"complete or improve through compatibility"}],"section":5,"initial":"c"},{"id":210,"targetId":210,"type":"tc","text":"The intercepted messages showed that the firms did more than independently raise prices: they agreed to {{1}} to exclude new competitors.","choices":[["conspire","diverge","capitulate","disperse","improvise"]],"answers":[["conspire"]],"explanation":"An agreement to coordinate exclusion secretly is conspiracy. Diverge and disperse mean moving apart, while capitulate and improvise do not express collusion.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":5,"initial":"c"},{"id":211,"targetId":211,"type":"tc","text":"Once thought to be a contaminant, the mineral proved to be a normal {{1}} of the rock, present in every uncontaminated sample.","choices":[["constituent","antagonist","precursor","substitute","anomaly"]],"answers":[["constituent"]],"explanation":"Presence in all clean samples establishes that the mineral is a component, or constituent. It is neither an anomaly nor merely something preceding the rock.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":5,"initial":"c"},{"id":212,"targetId":212,"type":"tc","text":"It would be a mistake to {{1}} the author’s silence as approval; her private letters are {{2}} to the policy she declined to discuss publicly.","choices":[["construe","enforce","suspend"],["antithetical","analogous","incidental"]],"answers":[["construe"],["antithetical"]],"explanation":"Construe means interpret. The warning against reading silence as approval is supported by letters fundamentally opposed, or antithetical, to the policy.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[],"section":5,"initial":"c"},{"id":213,"targetId":213,"type":"se","text":"The reviewer’s sneering references to the poet’s provincial origins revealed {{1}}, not merely a reasoned disagreement with the poems.","choices":[["contempt","scorn","curiosity","solicitude","reverence","gratitude"]],"answers":[["contempt","scorn"]],"explanation":"Sneering at someone’s origins expresses disdainful disrespect, captured by contempt and scorn. None of the other attitudes explains the belittling tone.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":5,"initial":"c"},{"id":214,"targetId":214,"type":"tc","text":"The critics {{1}} that the project was badly managed, explicitly advancing that claim even while acknowledging its workers’ admirable performance.","choices":[["contend","conceal","retract","deny","doubt"]],"answers":[["contend"]],"explanation":"Explicitly advancing a claim means asserting it, or contending that it is true. Concealing, retracting, denying, and doubting the claim would not amount to advancing it.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":5,"initial":"c"},{"id":215,"targetId":215,"type":"tc","text":"The appointment was so {{1}} that even a routine vote required hours of debate; the chair’s expectation of immediate approval proved {{2}}.","choices":[["contentious","uncontroversial","perfunctory"],["erroneous","prescient","justified"]],"answers":[["contentious"],["erroneous"]],"explanation":"Prolonged disagreement makes the appointment contentious. The expectation of immediate approval was mistaken, or erroneous, rather than confirmed.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"erroneous","meaning":"mistaken or incorrect"}],"section":5,"initial":"c"},{"id":216,"targetId":216,"type":"tc","text":"The donation was {{1}} on the city maintaining free admission, so the trustees could not introduce a ticket charge without forfeiting the funds.","choices":[["contingent","invariant","unconditional","extraneous","retrospective"]],"answers":[["contingent"]],"explanation":"The donation depends on a condition, making it contingent. Unconditional and invariant conflict with the risk of losing funds.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":5,"initial":"c"},{"id":217,"targetId":217,"type":"tc","text":"The new directive would {{1}} an existing statute, despite the minister’s claim of {{2}} with the law; that contradiction made judicial {{3}} almost inevitable.","choices":[["contravene","codify","clarify"],["compliance","conflict","disagreement"],["scrutiny","indifference","endorsement"]],"answers":[["contravene"],["compliance"],["scrutiny"]],"explanation":"The directive violates, or contravenes, the statute. Compliance is the minister’s contrary claim, and the conflict invites examination by courts, or scrutiny.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"compliance","meaning":"acting in accordance with a requirement"},{"word":"scrutiny","meaning":"close critical examination"}],"section":5,"initial":"c"},{"id":218,"targetId":218,"type":"se","text":"The mistaken seating arrangement caused a brief {{1}} between the guests, but apologies restored the evening’s friendly atmosphere.","choices":[["contretemps","altercation","celebration","reconciliation","alliance","accord"]],"answers":[["contretemps","altercation"]],"explanation":"The seating mistake produces a short awkward dispute: a contretemps or altercation. Apologies resolve the dispute; they do not initiate an alliance or celebration.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"altercation","meaning":"a heated disagreement"}],"section":5,"initial":"c"},{"id":219,"targetId":219,"type":"tc","text":"His apology sounded {{1}}, but its sincerity remained doubtful because he offered no {{2}} for the damage and repeated the same conduct a week later.","choices":[["contrite","defiant","jubilant"],["restitution","pretext","gratification"]],"answers":[["contrite"],["restitution"]],"explanation":"An apology can sound remorseful, or contrite, without genuine change. Failure to make restitution means no compensation was offered for the harm.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"restitution","meaning":"compensation or restoration for harm"}],"section":5,"initial":"c"},{"id":220,"targetId":220,"type":"tc","text":"The judge distinguished the defendant’s fear of punishment from genuine {{1}}: regretting capture was not the same as regretting the harm inflicted.","choices":[["contrition","trepidation","calculation","indignation","exhaustion"]],"answers":[["contrition"]],"explanation":"The distinction is between fear and sincere remorse. Contrition names regret for wrongdoing; trepidation merely repeats the fear of punishment.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":5,"initial":"c"},{"id":221,"targetId":221,"type":"tc","text":"Unable to obtain permission for a direct interview, the journalist had to {{1}} a way of meeting the official at a public reception.","choices":[["contrive","abandon","prohibit","disclose","obstruct"]],"answers":[["contrive"]],"explanation":"The journalist must devise a clever indirect approach, which is to contrive one. The other verbs do not mean inventing a workable method.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":5,"initial":"c"},{"id":222,"targetId":222,"type":"tc","text":"The physician described the patient as {{1}}, neither fully recovered nor acutely ill, and recommended a return to work that was {{2}} rather than abrupt.","choices":[["convalescent","invulnerable","moribund"],["gradual","instantaneous","compulsory"]],"answers":[["convalescent"],["gradual"]],"explanation":"Someone recovering from illness is convalescent. The contrast with abrupt requires a gradual return, allowing recovery to continue.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"gradual","meaning":"developing in small stages"}],"section":5,"initial":"c"},{"id":223,"targetId":223,"type":"se","text":"Despite its futuristic setting, the film’s plot is thoroughly {{1}}, following a familiar rescue narrative without a single unexpected turn.","choices":[["conventional","formulaic","radical","incoherent","fragmentary","subversive"]],"answers":[["conventional","formulaic"]],"explanation":"A familiar, predictable narrative is both conventional and formulaic. Futuristic scenery does not make its plot radical or subversive.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"formulaic","meaning":"following a predictable established pattern"}],"section":5,"initial":"c"},{"id":224,"targetId":224,"type":"tc","text":"Her willingness to defend the theory after it became unfashionable suggested that her earlier endorsement reflected {{1}} rather than a desire for popularity.","choices":[["conviction","expediency","indecision","affectation","deference"]],"answers":[["conviction"]],"explanation":"Persisting despite social costs supports firm belief, or conviction. Expediency and a desire for popularity would instead favor abandoning the unfashionable view.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":5,"initial":"c"},{"id":225,"targetId":225,"type":"tc","text":"The laboratory’s {{1}} lunches encouraged lively conversation, but the director resisted making attendance mandatory: sociability achieved through {{2}} would defeat the purpose.","choices":[["convivial","solemn","clandestine"],["coercion","spontaneity","consensus"]],"answers":[["convivial"],["coercion"]],"explanation":"Lively social lunches are convivial. Compulsory attendance would involve coercion, undermining the voluntary friendliness the meals seek.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[],"section":5,"initial":"c"},{"id":226,"targetId":226,"type":"tc","text":"The explanation became so {{1}} that a diagram intended to clarify it required a second diagram explaining the first.","choices":[["convoluted","lucid","succinct","elementary","linear"]],"answers":[["convoluted"]],"explanation":"An explanation needing further explanations is excessively intricate, or convoluted. Lucid, succinct, elementary, and linear all suggest easier comprehension.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":5,"initial":"c"},{"id":227,"targetId":227,"type":"tc","text":"The excavation produced {{1}} records, yet their quantity concealed a lack of {{2}}: because the reports contradicted one another, their conclusions were {{3}}.","choices":[["copious","scant","negligible"],["consistency","variety","detail"],["inconclusive","definitive","unassailable"]],"answers":[["copious"],["consistency"],["inconclusive"]],"explanation":"Copious indicates many records. Contradictions show missing consistency, and prevent a settled result, making the conclusions inconclusive.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"consistency","meaning":"agreement between statements or results"},{"word":"inconclusive","meaning":"not establishing a firm result"}],"section":5,"initial":"c"},{"id":228,"targetId":228,"type":"se","text":"Although the negotiations failed, the delegates remained {{1}}, exchanging warm thanks and invitations rather than accusations.","choices":[["cordial","amiable","hostile","aloof","suspicious","vindictive"]],"answers":[["cordial","amiable"]],"explanation":"Warm, friendly conduct fits both cordial and amiable. Failed negotiations did not produce hostility, distance, suspicion, or revenge.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"amiable","meaning":"friendly and pleasant"}],"section":5,"initial":"c"},{"id":229,"targetId":229,"type":"tc","text":"The philosopher distinguished the mind’s abstract operations from the {{1}} conditions of thought, insisting that fatigue and hunger were hardly {{2}} to reasoning.","choices":[["corporeal","ethereal","hypothetical"],["irrelevant","indispensable","synonymous"]],"answers":[["corporeal"],["irrelevant"]],"explanation":"Fatigue and hunger are bodily, hence corporeal. Saying they are hardly irrelevant asserts their importance without claiming that they are indispensable or identical to reasoning.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"irrelevant","meaning":"unconnected to the matter at hand"}],"section":5,"initial":"c"},{"id":230,"targetId":230,"type":"tc","text":"Two measures may {{1}} closely without either causing the other; both could respond to an unmeasured third factor.","choices":[["correlate","conflict","oscillate","dissipate","diverge"]],"answers":[["correlate"]],"explanation":"The warning distinguishes statistical association from causation. Correlate describes measures varying together; the other choices do not express that relationship.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":5,"initial":"c"},{"id":231,"targetId":231,"type":"tc","text":"The new witness could {{1}} the cyclist’s account because her independently recorded video showed the same sequence of events.","choices":[["corroborate","repudiate","embellish","obscure","parody"]],"answers":[["corroborate"]],"explanation":"Independent matching evidence confirms the account, so corroborate fits. Embellish adds details without necessarily verifying them; the others contradict or distort it.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":5,"initial":"c"},{"id":232,"targetId":232,"type":"tc","text":"Her upbringing in several countries made her outlook {{1}} rather than parochial; customs others found bewildering she approached with informed {{2}}.","choices":[["cosmopolitan","insular","doctrinaire"],["curiosity","contempt","aversion"]],"answers":[["cosmopolitan"],["curiosity"]],"explanation":"Familiarity with different cultures is cosmopolitan. Informed curiosity suits openness, unlike contempt or aversion, which would imply rejection.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"curiosity","meaning":"interest in discovering or understanding"}],"section":5,"initial":"c"},{"id":233,"targetId":233,"type":"se","text":"The committee would not {{1}} the suppression of unfavorable results, insisting on full publication even when disclosure threatened its own reputation.","choices":[["countenance","tolerate","investigate","publicize","forestall","lament"]],"answers":[["countenance","tolerate"]],"explanation":"The committee refuses to permit suppression: countenance and tolerate have that sense. Investigating, exposing, preventing, or regretting suppression are different responses.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"tolerate","meaning":"allow or put up with"}],"section":5,"initial":"c"},{"id":234,"targetId":234,"type":"tc","text":"Because open sponsorship would compromise the researchers’ independence, the company attempted to exert influence through {{1}} payments routed through intermediaries.","choices":[["covert","transparent","nominal","mandatory","ceremonial"]],"answers":[["covert"]],"explanation":"Routing payments through intermediaries hides their source, making them covert. Transparent is the opposite; small, compulsory, or ceremonial payments need not be hidden.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":5,"initial":"c"},{"id":235,"targetId":235,"type":"tc","text":"Though publicly dismissing the award as trivial, the novelist continued to {{1}} it; her elaborate indifference was an {{2}} designed to conceal disappointment.","choices":[["covet","forfeit","disparage"],["artifice","impediment","exposition"]],"answers":[["covet"],["artifice"]],"explanation":"She secretly desires the award, or covets it. The performed indifference is a deceptive device, an artifice, rather than a barrier or explanation.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"artifice","meaning":"a contrived deceptive device"}],"section":5,"initial":"c"},{"id":236,"targetId":236,"type":"tc","text":"Usually eager to discuss his research, the scientist became unexpectedly {{1}} about the discovery, hinting at its importance while refusing to reveal any details.","choices":[["coy","forthcoming","indignant","despondent","garrulous"]],"answers":[["coy"]],"explanation":"Suggestive reluctance to disclose is coyness. Forthcoming and garrulous predict more disclosure; indignation and despondency are not supported.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":5,"initial":"c"},{"id":237,"targetId":237,"type":"tc","text":"The commander’s refusal to send troops into an obvious ambush was prudent, not {{1}}; confusing {{2}} with cowardice would {{3}} reckless sacrifice.","choices":[["craven","judicious","cautious"],["circumspection","bravado","negligence"],["encourage","forestall","condemn"]],"answers":[["craven"],["circumspection"],["encourage"]],"explanation":"The refusal is not shamefully cowardly, or craven. Circumspection is careful judgment, and treating it as cowardice would encourage needless risk-taking.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"circumspection","meaning":"careful consideration of possible consequences"},{"word":"encourage","meaning":"promote or give support to"}],"section":5,"initial":"c"},{"id":238,"targetId":238,"type":"se","text":"Once the laboratory independently reproduced the result, a claim previously dismissed as fantasy became {{1}}.","choices":[["credible","plausible","fanciful","irrelevant","obsolete","opaque"]],"answers":[["credible","plausible"]],"explanation":"Successful replication makes the claim believable: credible or plausible. It does not make it fanciful, irrelevant, outdated, or harder to understand.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"plausible","meaning":"apparently believable or reasonable"}],"section":5,"initial":"c"},{"id":239,"targetId":239,"type":"tc","text":"The team was {{1}} after losing the final, but the coach’s praise gradually restored the {{2}} that defeat had extinguished.","choices":[["crestfallen","jubilant","indifferent"],["enthusiasm","resentment","complacency"]],"answers":[["crestfallen"],["enthusiasm"]],"explanation":"Defeat leaves the team dejected, or crestfallen. Praise restores enthusiasm; resentment and complacency would not represent the constructive recovery described.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"enthusiasm","meaning":"eager interest or excitement"}],"section":5,"initial":"c"},{"id":240,"targetId":240,"type":"tc","text":"Rather than project her voice across the hall, the singer chose to {{1}} into the microphone, creating an intimate atmosphere through a soft, gentle delivery.","choices":[["croon","bellow","declaim","shriek","exhort"]],"answers":[["croon"]],"explanation":"A soft, intimate singing style is crooning. Bellowing, declaiming, and shrieking suggest forceful projection; exhorting means urging.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":5,"initial":"c"},{"id":241,"targetId":241,"type":"tc","text":"The inscription’s brevity did not make it accessible: its allusions were so {{1}} that specialists disagreed even about its subject.","choices":[["cryptic","explicit","redundant","didactic","prosaic"]],"answers":[["cryptic"]],"explanation":"Disagreement over the basic subject shows mysterious, hard-to-decipher allusions. Cryptic fits; brevity does not guarantee explicit or plain language.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":5,"initial":"c"},{"id":242,"targetId":242,"type":"tc","text":"Years of painstaking restoration would {{1}} in the building’s reopening, an event the trustees viewed as the project’s {{2}} rather than merely another interim stage.","choices":[["culminate","originate","fragment"],["acme","impediment","digression"]],"answers":[["culminate"],["acme"]],"explanation":"Culminate means reach a final or highest point. Acme names that peak, in contrast to an interim stage, obstacle, or departure from the main task.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"acme","meaning":"the highest point"}],"section":5,"initial":"c"},{"id":243,"targetId":243,"type":"se","text":"Because the investigator had ordered the records destroyed, his {{1}} could not be dismissed as the mere error of a subordinate.","choices":[["culpability","blameworthiness","ingenuity","immunity","credulity","obscurity"]],"answers":[["culpability","blameworthiness"]],"explanation":"Ordering destruction establishes responsibility for wrongdoing, captured by culpability and blameworthiness. The alternatives do not describe moral responsibility.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"blameworthiness","meaning":"deserving responsibility for wrongdoing"}],"section":5,"initial":"c"},{"id":244,"targetId":244,"type":"tc","text":"Each minor purchase required signatures from nine offices, making the authorization process so {{1}} that departments sometimes abandoned necessary purchases altogether.","choices":[["cumbersome","expeditious","discretionary","informal","intuitive"]],"answers":[["cumbersome"]],"explanation":"Numerous required approvals make the process unwieldy and difficult to manage: cumbersome. The other choices suggest speed, flexibility, or ease.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":5,"initial":"c"},{"id":245,"targetId":245,"type":"tc","text":"The negotiator relied on {{1}} rather than force, securing concessions through misleading hints whose apparent innocence concealed calculated {{2}}.","choices":[["cunning","candor","lethargy"],["guile","probity","naivety"]],"answers":[["cunning"],["guile"]],"explanation":"Misleading hints indicate skillful deception, or cunning. Guile reinforces the concealed craftiness; probity and naivety contradict calculated deceit.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"guile","meaning":"deceptive cleverness"}],"section":5,"initial":"c"},{"id":246,"targetId":246,"type":"tc","text":"The policy was meant to {{1}} speculation by making rapid resale costly, not to eliminate ordinary investment.","choices":[["curb","stimulate","vindicate","conceal","subsidize"]],"answers":[["curb"]],"explanation":"Making resale costly restrains speculation, so curb fits. Stimulate and subsidize would encourage the behavior the policy aims to limit.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":5,"initial":"c"},{"id":247,"targetId":247,"type":"tc","text":"Initially dismissed as a {{1}}, the critic had a legitimate complaint; unfortunately, his habitual {{2}} obscured it, and listeners heard only another expression of {{3}}.","choices":[["curmudgeon","sycophant","visionary"],["grumbling","flattery","reticence"],["discontent","gratitude","serenity"]],"answers":[["curmudgeon"],["grumbling"],["discontent"]],"explanation":"An irritable habitual complainer is a curmudgeon. His grumbling makes the valid point sound like routine discontent, rather than gratitude or calm.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"grumbling","meaning":"habitual low-voiced complaining"},{"word":"discontent","meaning":"dissatisfaction with existing circumstances"}],"section":5,"initial":"c"},{"id":248,"targetId":248,"type":"se","text":"A {{1}} inspection missed the damaged seal because the examiner glanced at the instrument for only a few seconds.","choices":[["cursory","perfunctory","exhaustive","meticulous","intrusive","protracted"]],"answers":[["cursory","perfunctory"]],"explanation":"A brief, superficial inspection is cursory or perfunctory. Exhaustive and meticulous imply care; intrusive concerns interference and protracted concerns duration.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":5,"initial":"c"},{"id":249,"targetId":249,"type":"tc","text":"Officials decided to {{1}} the festival after the storm warning, treating its final day as {{2}} rather than exposing audiences to the approaching gale.","choices":[["curtail","prolong","inaugurate"],["dispensable","inevitable","inviolable"]],"answers":[["curtail"],["dispensable"]],"explanation":"Curtail means shorten. Canceling the last day treats it as dispensable, something that can be omitted, not as inevitable or untouchable.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"dispensable","meaning":"able to be omitted or sacrificed"}],"section":5,"initial":"c"},{"id":250,"targetId":250,"type":"tc","text":"The archive’s sheer scale was {{1}} even to experienced historians: cataloging its contents would require several lifetimes of individual work.","choices":[["daunting","trivial","reassuring","familiar","negligible"]],"answers":[["daunting"]],"explanation":"A task requiring lifetimes is intimidating in difficulty, or daunting. Experience does not make that enormous scale trivial or negligible.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":5,"initial":"d"},{"id":251,"targetId":251,"type":"tc","text":"The travelers had spent the morning stopping idly every few steps, but with the last ferry about to leave they could no longer afford to {{1}}.","choices":[["dawdle","hasten","sprint","march","accelerate"]],"answers":[["dawdle"]],"explanation":"Idle stops establish wasted time, and the departing ferry makes further delay costly. Dawdle means waste time or move slowly; all the alternatives imply purposeful or faster movement.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":6,"initial":"d"},{"id":252,"targetId":252,"type":"tc","text":"The apparent {{1}} of evidence reflected poor cataloging rather than empty archives; an {{2}} search revealed hundreds of relevant letters.","choices":[["dearth","abundance","redundancy"],["assiduous","indolent","erratic"]],"answers":[["dearth"],["assiduous"]],"explanation":"Evidence initially seemed scarce, a dearth. A diligent, persistent search is assiduous and explains why overlooked documents were recovered.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"assiduous","meaning":"persistently diligent"}],"section":6,"initial":"d"},{"id":253,"targetId":253,"type":"se","text":"Even after the fever subsided, the illness remained {{1}}, leaving the researcher unable to climb a single flight of stairs.","choices":[["debilitating","enervating","invigorating","restorative","intermittent","contagious"]],"answers":[["debilitating","enervating"]],"explanation":"Inability to climb stairs indicates weakness, making debilitating and enervating equivalent. The passage does not establish contagiousness or intermittence, while the other pair implies renewed strength.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"enervating","meaning":"draining strength or vitality"}],"section":6,"initial":"d"},{"id":254,"targetId":254,"type":"tc","text":"By reconstructing the supposedly miraculous trick with ordinary mirrors, the investigator was able to {{1}} the claim that supernatural powers were involved.","choices":[["debunk","endorse","embroider","propagate","sanctify"]],"answers":[["debunk"]],"explanation":"An ordinary physical explanation exposes the supernatural claim as false. Debunk fits; the other choices would support, embellish, spread, or honor it.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":6,"initial":"d"},{"id":255,"targetId":255,"type":"tc","text":"The austere reformer regarded the court as {{1}}, condemning its endless feasts as an expression of {{2}} rather than public service.","choices":[["decadent","abstemious","frugal"],["hedonism","altruism","asceticism"]],"answers":[["decadent"],["hedonism"]],"explanation":"Excessive luxury makes the court decadent. Hedonism, pursuit of pleasure, explains the reformer’s objection; altruism and asceticism conflict with the lavish behavior.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"hedonism","meaning":"devotion to pleasure"}],"section":6,"initial":"d"},{"id":256,"targetId":256,"type":"tc","text":"Although the surviving marks were visible, scholars could not {{1}} them until a bilingual inscription supplied the missing key.","choices":[["decipher","preserve","photograph","catalog","excavate"]],"answers":[["decipher"]],"explanation":"A bilingual key enables decoding rather than merely seeing or recording marks. Decipher is the only option that describes recovering their meaning.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":6,"initial":"d"},{"id":257,"targetId":257,"type":"tc","text":"The ambassador remained {{1}} throughout the insulting speech, displaying none of the {{2}} her aides feared; afterward, however, her {{3}} private response made her anger unmistakable.","choices":[["decorous","boorish","raucous"],["petulance","composure","restraint"],["apoplectic","equable","indifferent"]],"answers":[["decorous"],["petulance"],["apoplectic"]],"explanation":"Decorous means outwardly proper. Petulance is the ill-tempered reaction she suppresses publicly; an apoplectic private response reveals intense anger rather than calm.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"petulance","meaning":"irritable childish ill temper"},{"word":"apoplectic","meaning":"extremely angry"}],"section":6,"initial":"d"},{"id":258,"targetId":258,"type":"se","text":"The audience’s hushed attention and refusal to interrupt maintained the {{1}} expected at the solemn memorial.","choices":[["decorum","propriety","frivolity","acrimony","disorder","levity"]],"answers":[["decorum","propriety"]],"explanation":"Respectful conduct at a solemn event displays decorum or propriety. The other choices suggest inappropriate lightness, hostility, or disorder.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":6,"initial":"d"},{"id":259,"targetId":259,"type":"tc","text":"The editorial did more than question the decision: it chose to {{1}} it in language so {{2}} that readers could not mistake the newspaper’s opposition.","choices":[["decry","ratify","obscure"],["vehement","equivocal","dispassionate"]],"answers":[["decry"],["vehement"]],"explanation":"Decry means condemn publicly. Vehement language conveys forceful opposition, unlike ambiguity or emotionally detached assessment.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"vehement","meaning":"forceful and strongly expressed"}],"section":6,"initial":"d"},{"id":260,"targetId":260,"type":"tc","text":"Despite her senior rank, the geologist showed {{1}} to the local guide on matters of terrain, recognizing expertise that formal credentials did not capture.","choices":[["deference","antipathy","indifference","suspicion","condescension"]],"answers":[["deference"]],"explanation":"Respectfully yielding to the guide’s expertise is deference. Condescension would imply looking down on him, contrary to recognizing his authority.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":6,"initial":"d"},{"id":261,"targetId":261,"type":"tc","text":"The apprentice was attentive without being {{1}}: she listened carefully but challenged the master whenever the evidence warranted it.","choices":[["deferential","observant","independent","inquisitive","diligent"]],"answers":[["deferential"]],"explanation":"The contrast is between attention and submission to authority. Deferential conveys yielding respect; evidence-based challenges show that she did not simply defer.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":6,"initial":"d"},{"id":262,"targetId":262,"type":"tc","text":"Asked about the missing funds, the treasurer tried to {{1}} attention toward a minor clerical error, a tactic that increased rather than diminished the investigators’ {{2}}.","choices":[["deflect","concentrate","fix"],["suspicion","satisfaction","credulity"]],"answers":[["deflect"],["suspicion"]],"explanation":"Deflect means redirect attention away from the central issue. An evasive diversion makes investigators more suspicious, not more satisfied or gullible.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"suspicion","meaning":"a sense that something is wrong"}],"section":6,"initial":"d"},{"id":263,"targetId":263,"type":"se","text":"With a {{1}} adjustment of two screws, the technician restored the delicate mechanism in seconds, accomplishing what others had failed to do in hours.","choices":[["deft","adroit","clumsy","tentative","accidental","lethargic"]],"answers":[["deft","adroit"]],"explanation":"A quick, skillful repair is deft or adroit. Clumsy, tentative, accidental, and lethargic do not describe demonstrated precision and competence.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":6,"initial":"d"},{"id":264,"targetId":264,"type":"tc","text":"The biography refuses to {{1}} its subject: it admires her achievements while recording her selfishness and serious errors of judgment.","choices":[["deify","humanize","criticize","contextualize","scrutinize"]],"answers":[["deify"]],"explanation":"Presenting flaws resists making the subject godlike or infallible. Deify fits; humanizing, criticizing, contextualizing, and scrutinizing are compatible with the biography’s approach.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":6,"initial":"d"},{"id":265,"targetId":265,"type":"tc","text":"The drainage scheme’s {{1}} effects were initially invisible, but later crop failures showed that the reassuring early report had been {{2}}.","choices":[["deleterious","salutary","negligible"],["premature","prescient","exhaustive"]],"answers":[["deleterious"],["premature"]],"explanation":"Crop failures reveal harmful, or deleterious, consequences. Early reassurance issued before those consequences appeared was premature rather than foresighted or comprehensive.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"premature","meaning":"occurring before sufficient time or evidence"}],"section":6,"initial":"d"},{"id":266,"targetId":266,"type":"tc","text":"The omission was {{1}}, not an oversight: correspondence showed that the editor had explicitly instructed assistants to remove the passage.","choices":[["deliberate","inadvertent","fortuitous","inexplicable","unnoticed"]],"answers":[["deliberate"]],"explanation":"Explicit instructions establish intentional omission. Deliberate contrasts with oversight; inadvertent and fortuitous would imply accident.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":6,"initial":"d"},{"id":267,"targetId":267,"type":"tc","text":"The report should {{1}} the agencies’ responsibilities, replacing vague overlap with {{2}} boundaries; otherwise, each agency can {{3}} accountability by blaming the other.","choices":[["delineate","conflate","obscure"],["discrete","porous","indistinct"],["evade","embrace","enforce"]],"answers":[["delineate"],["discrete"],["evade"]],"explanation":"Delineate means define precisely. Discrete responsibilities are distinct, while unclear overlap enables agencies to evade, or avoid, accountability.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[],"section":6,"initial":"d"},{"id":268,"targetId":268,"type":"se","text":"Her certainty that the sealed machine could read thoughts was a {{1}}, sustained despite repeated demonstrations that it contained no working parts.","choices":[["delusion","misapprehension","discovery","revelation","hypothesis","verification"]],"answers":[["delusion","misapprehension"]],"explanation":"A persistent false belief is a delusion or misapprehension. Discovery, revelation, and verification suggest truth; a hypothesis is tentative, unlike her resistant certainty.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"misapprehension","meaning":"a mistaken belief or understanding"}],"section":6,"initial":"d"},{"id":269,"targetId":269,"type":"tc","text":"Although usually compliant, the analyst felt compelled to {{1}} when asked to certify figures she knew were false; refusing the request was a matter of {{2}}.","choices":[["demur","acquiesce","collaborate"],["probity","expediency","vanity"]],"answers":[["demur"],["probity"]],"explanation":"Demur means raise an objection. Refusing to endorse false figures expresses honesty, or probity, rather than convenience or self-display.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[],"section":6,"initial":"d"},{"id":270,"targetId":270,"type":"tc","text":"The critic’s habit of using an artist’s humble origins to {{1}} the work substitutes social prejudice for aesthetic judgment.","choices":[["denigrate","authenticate","commission","preserve","explicate"]],"answers":[["denigrate"]],"explanation":"Using origins to belittle artwork is denigration. The critic is not verifying, ordering, preserving, or explaining the work.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":6,"initial":"d"},{"id":271,"targetId":271,"type":"tc","text":"In this edition, a dagger beside a date does not {{1}} uncertainty; it marks an emendation supported by a newly discovered document.","choices":[["denote","eliminate","resolve","increase","justify"]],"answers":[["denote"]],"explanation":"The question concerns what a symbol signifies. Denote is the verb for indicating meaning; the other choices describe effects on uncertainty rather than notation.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":6,"initial":"d"},{"id":272,"targetId":272,"type":"tc","text":"The mayor was quick to {{1}} corruption in others, yet her silence about her own allies made that posture appear {{2}}.","choices":[["denounce","excuse","conceal"],["hypocritical","evenhanded","ingenuous"]],"answers":[["denounce"],["hypocritical"]],"explanation":"Denounce means condemn openly. Condemning opponents while sparing allies is inconsistent and hypocritical, not impartial or innocently candid.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"hypocritical","meaning":"contradicting one's professed standards"}],"section":6,"initial":"d"},{"id":273,"targetId":273,"type":"se","text":"The tutor praised the student’s {{1}}, noting that she greeted visitors politely, listened without interrupting, and carried herself with quiet assurance.","choices":[["deportment","demeanor","erudition","lineage","eloquence","dexterity"]],"answers":[["deportment","demeanor"]],"explanation":"The examples concern outward behavior and bearing, captured by deportment and demeanor. They do not demonstrate ancestry, scholarship, persuasive speech, or manual skill.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"demeanor","meaning":"outward manner and bearing"}],"section":6,"initial":"d"},{"id":274,"targetId":274,"type":"tc","text":"When the ruler attempted to dissolve the legislature permanently, a coalition formed to {{1}} him and install a constitutional government.","choices":[["depose","enthrone","canonize","placate","emulate"]],"answers":[["depose"]],"explanation":"Replacing a ruler requires removing him from office, or deposing him. Enthrone would install him; the other verbs do not mean removal.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":6,"initial":"d"},{"id":275,"targetId":275,"type":"tc","text":"The essay does not {{1}} ambition itself; it objects only to ambition unrestrained by {{2}} for the welfare of others.","choices":[["deprecate","cultivate","exemplify"],["concern","contempt","indifference"]],"answers":[["deprecate"],["concern"]],"explanation":"Deprecate means express disapproval. The essay accepts ambition when tempered by concern for others, unlike contempt or indifference.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"concern","meaning":"care for someone's welfare"}],"section":6,"initial":"d"},{"id":276,"targetId":276,"type":"tc","text":"Rather than answer the young scholar’s argument, senior colleagues chose to {{1}} her accent, exposing the weakness of their intellectual response.","choices":[["deride","imitate","analyze","commend","normalize"]],"answers":[["deride"]],"explanation":"Mocking an accent instead of answering an argument is derision. Imitation need not be contemptuous, while analysis and commendation do not convey ridicule.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":6,"initial":"d"},{"id":277,"targetId":277,"type":"tc","text":"Although advertised as revolutionary, the design was {{1}}: its central features came from an established {{2}}, and its claim to originality depended on readers’ {{3}} of that tradition.","choices":[["derivative","unprecedented","autonomous"],["canon","anomaly","impasse"],["ignorance","mastery","endorsement"]],"answers":[["derivative"],["canon"],["ignorance"]],"explanation":"Borrowing established features makes a work derivative. A canon is an accepted body of works or principles; only ignorance of that source permits the false originality claim.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"canon","meaning":"an accepted body of works or principles"},{"word":"ignorance","meaning":"lack of knowledge"}],"section":6,"initial":"d"},{"id":278,"targetId":278,"type":"se","text":"The composer chose to {{1}} the melody from a local folk tune, openly acknowledging the source rather than claiming spontaneous invention.","choices":[["derive","extract","obliterate","suppress","disown","rebut"]],"answers":[["derive","extract"]],"explanation":"Derive and extract both mean obtain the melody from a source. The remaining verbs reject, destroy, or conceal rather than draw material from it.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"extract","meaning":"obtain or draw out"}],"section":6,"initial":"d"},{"id":279,"targetId":279,"type":"tc","text":"To preserve the specimen, technicians would {{1}} it, reducing its moisture content until microbial growth became {{2}}.","choices":[["desiccate","saturate","dissolve"],["unlikely","inevitable","vigorous"]],"answers":[["desiccate"],["unlikely"]],"explanation":"Desiccate means dry thoroughly. Removing moisture discourages microbial growth, making it unlikely rather than inevitable or vigorous.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"unlikely","meaning":"not expected to happen"}],"section":6,"initial":"d"},{"id":280,"targetId":280,"type":"tc","text":"The governor’s insistence that no court could review his decrees revealed a {{1}} conception of authority, incompatible with constitutional limits.","choices":[["despotic","pluralistic","tentative","egalitarian","conciliatory"]],"answers":[["despotic"]],"explanation":"Unreviewable decrees imply oppressive absolute power, or despotism. Pluralistic and egalitarian government distribute power; tentative and conciliatory authority is not absolute.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":6,"initial":"d"},{"id":281,"targetId":281,"type":"tc","text":"The committee’s inquiry was {{1}}, shifting from one unrelated topic to another without pursuing any lead to its conclusion.","choices":[["desultory","methodical","exhaustive","relentless","coherent"]],"answers":[["desultory"]],"explanation":"Unconnected shifts and unfinished leads reveal a lack of sustained purpose, or desultoriness. The alternatives imply organized or persistent inquiry.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":6,"initial":"d"},{"id":282,"targetId":282,"type":"tc","text":"The exchange of ambassadors signaled a {{1}}, but it did not erase the longstanding {{2}} between the states; reduced hostility was not yet friendship.","choices":[["detente","rupture","conflagration"],["enmity","affinity","solidarity"]],"answers":[["detente"],["enmity"]],"explanation":"Detente is an easing of hostility. Longstanding enmity can remain despite that improvement, which is why the passage distinguishes reduced tension from friendship.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[],"section":6,"initial":"d"},{"id":283,"targetId":283,"type":"se","text":"The replication team refused to {{1}} from the published procedure, knowing that any departure would complicate comparison with the original results.","choices":[["deviate","depart","benefit","recover","extrapolate","recoil"]],"answers":[["deviate","depart"]],"explanation":"Deviate from and depart from both mean leave the specified course. The point is procedural fidelity, not recovery, benefit, extrapolation, or emotional withdrawal.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"depart","meaning":"move away from a course"}],"section":6,"initial":"d"},{"id":284,"targetId":284,"type":"tc","text":"What began as an assessment of the proposal deteriorated into a {{1}} against its author, with denunciations replacing substantive analysis.","choices":[["diatribe","eulogy","synopsis","compromise","dialogue"]],"answers":[["diatribe"]],"explanation":"A lengthy bitter attack is a diatribe. Eulogy is praise, synopsis is summary, and compromise or dialogue would imply a more constructive exchange.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":6,"initial":"d"},{"id":285,"targetId":285,"type":"tc","text":"The supposed {{1}} between tradition and innovation is misleading: artists can transform inherited forms without {{2}} them entirely.","choices":[["dichotomy","continuity","alliance"],["abandoning","preserving","studying"]],"answers":[["dichotomy"],["abandoning"]],"explanation":"Dichotomy presents two categories as sharply opposed. Transformation need not involve abandoning tradition, so the passage rejects the forced choice.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"abandoning","meaning":"giving up or leaving behind"}],"section":6,"initial":"d"},{"id":286,"targetId":286,"type":"tc","text":"A small donor should not be allowed to {{1}} an entire museum’s exhibition policy merely because one acquisition depended on her gift.","choices":[["dictate","question","observe","summarize","misinterpret"]],"answers":[["dictate"]],"explanation":"The objection concerns controlling policy, which is to dictate it. A donor can question or observe policy without exercising the impermissible control described.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":6,"initial":"d"},{"id":287,"targetId":287,"type":"tc","text":"The director called his approach collaborative, but his {{1}} manner made dissent impossible; the consultation was {{2}}, and employees’ apparent agreement reflected {{3}} rather than conviction.","choices":[["dictatorial","deferential","diffident"],["illusory","substantive","reciprocal"],["duress","enthusiasm","autonomy"]],"answers":[["dictatorial"],["illusory"],["duress"]],"explanation":"A dictatorial leader suppresses opposition. Consultation without real choice is illusory, and agreement under pressure reflects duress rather than independent belief.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[],"section":6,"initial":"d"},{"id":288,"targetId":288,"type":"se","text":"The fable is openly {{1}}, arranging every incident to teach readers that cleverness without compassion is ultimately self-defeating.","choices":[["didactic","instructive","aimless","equivocal","enigmatic","whimsical"]],"answers":[["didactic","instructive"]],"explanation":"An explicit moral lesson makes the fable didactic and instructive. Whimsy could describe tone but not the stated educational purpose; ambiguity and mystery conflict with its clarity.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"instructive","meaning":"providing knowledge or a lesson"}],"section":6,"initial":"d"},{"id":289,"targetId":289,"type":"tc","text":"Although her analysis was formidable, she remained {{1}} in meetings; only sustained encouragement helped her overcome this {{2}}.","choices":[["diffident","imperious","garrulous"],["diffidence","audacity","complacency"]],"answers":[["diffident"],["diffidence"]],"explanation":"The contrast is between strong analytical ability and weak confidence. Diffident describes the person, and diffidence names the self-doubt that encouragement addresses.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"diffidence","meaning":"lack of confidence"}],"section":6,"initial":"d"},{"id":290,"targetId":290,"type":"tc","text":"The lecture was so {{1}} that its central claim disappeared among digressions and qualifications, leaving listeners unsure what the speaker actually believed.","choices":[["diffuse","pithy","incisive","dogmatic","terse"]],"answers":[["diffuse"]],"explanation":"Diffuse here means wordy and unfocused. Numerous digressions obscure the thesis; pithy, incisive, and terse imply focus, while dogmatic suggests unmistakable assertions.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":6,"initial":"d"},{"id":291,"targetId":291,"type":"tc","text":"The chapter on medieval pigments might look like a {{1}}, but it becomes central when the author uses those pigments to date the disputed painting.","choices":[["digression","culmination","refutation","consensus","axiom"]],"answers":[["digression"]],"explanation":"Material initially seeming to leave the main subject looks like a digression. Its later relevance reverses that impression; the other nouns do not name an apparent departure.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":6,"initial":"d"},{"id":292,"targetId":292,"type":"tc","text":"The landlord’s repeated requests for already submitted documents were {{1}}, designed to postpone a ruling rather than improve its {{2}}.","choices":[["dilatory","expeditious","fortuitous"],["accuracy","lateness","redundancy"]],"answers":[["dilatory"],["accuracy"]],"explanation":"Requests intended to delay are dilatory. The contrast denies that they improve accuracy; lateness and redundancy instead describe consequences of delay.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"accuracy","meaning":"correctness and freedom from error"}],"section":6,"initial":"d"},{"id":293,"targetId":293,"type":"se","text":"The reviewer dismissed him as a {{1}}, noting that he collected the vocabulary of several disciplines without acquiring a serious command of any.","choices":[["dilettante","dabbler","polymath","specialist","virtuoso","authority"]],"answers":[["dilettante","dabbler"]],"explanation":"Superficial engagement in many fields describes a dilettante or dabbler. Polymath and the other alternatives imply real expertise, which the sentence explicitly denies.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"dabbler","meaning":"someone with a superficial interest"}],"section":6,"initial":"d"},{"id":294,"targetId":294,"type":"tc","text":"The manuscript’s {{1}} dimensions initially suggested a minor work, but its tiny pages contained an argument of extraordinary ambition.","choices":[["diminutive","monumental","indeterminate","conventional","variable"]],"answers":[["diminutive"]],"explanation":"Tiny pages establish very small dimensions, or diminutive ones. The intellectual ambition contrasts with physical size rather than uncertainty or variability.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":6,"initial":"d"},{"id":295,"targetId":295,"type":"tc","text":"The ceremony’s closing {{1}} transformed private grief into collective mourning; its descending phrases gave musical shape to an {{2}} for the dead.","choices":[["dirge","fanfare","revelry"],["elegy","invective","epigram"]],"answers":[["dirge"],["elegy"]],"explanation":"A dirge is mourning music. An elegy is an expression of mourning, often a poem; invective attacks and an epigram offers a concise witty thought.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"elegy","meaning":"a literary expression of mourning"}],"section":6,"initial":"d"},{"id":296,"targetId":296,"type":"tc","text":"The party’s most reliable organizers became {{1}} after leaders repeatedly ignored their concerns, and several began campaigning for its rivals.","choices":[["disaffected","reconciled","complacent","zealous","indebted"]],"answers":[["disaffected"]],"explanation":"Ignoring concerns produces lost loyalty, shown by campaigning for rivals. Disaffected captures that alienation; the other choices do not explain the defection.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":6,"initial":"d"},{"id":297,"targetId":297,"type":"tc","text":"Although the stain was barely {{1}}, improved imaging allowed conservators to {{2}} its edges; the resulting boundary was {{3}} enough to guide restoration.","choices":[["discernible","ubiquitous","ornamental"],["discern","obliterate","invent"],["distinct","illusory","irrelevant"]],"answers":[["discernible"],["discern"],["distinct"]],"explanation":"Discernible means detectable, and discern means perceive clearly. Imaging makes the edges distinct, yielding a usable boundary rather than an imagined or irrelevant one.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"discern","meaning":"recognize or perceive clearly"},{"word":"distinct","meaning":"clearly separate or recognizable"}],"section":6,"initial":"d"},{"id":298,"targetId":298,"type":"se","text":"The unexpected request for receipts seemed to {{1}} the administrator, whose easy confidence gave way to stammering and visible unease.","choices":[["discomfit","disconcert","reassure","gratify","vindicate","fortify"]],"answers":[["discomfit","disconcert"]],"explanation":"Visible unease and lost composure indicate embarrassment or disturbance: discomfit and disconcert. The other choices imply reassurance, pleasure, vindication, or support.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"disconcert","meaning":"unsettle or disturb someone's composure"}],"section":6,"initial":"d"},{"id":299,"targetId":299,"type":"tc","text":"The panel did not ignore the witness, but it chose to {{1}} his account after discovering that he stood to profit from the verdict; his interest compromised the expected {{2}}.","choices":[["discount","certify","amplify"],["detachment","eloquence","notoriety"]],"answers":[["discount"],["detachment"]],"explanation":"Discount means assign less evidentiary weight. A financial stake undermines impartial detachment, not necessarily speaking skill or public fame.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"detachment","meaning":"freedom from personal or emotional involvement"}],"section":6,"initial":"d"},{"id":300,"targetId":300,"type":"tc","text":"The archivist was {{1}} about the donor’s identity, discussing the papers’ importance freely while withholding every detail that might identify their owner.","choices":[["discreet","indiscriminate","garrulous","inquisitive","credulous"]],"answers":[["discreet"]],"explanation":"Careful protection of confidential identity is discreet behavior. Freely discussing the papers does not imply indiscriminate disclosure or gullibility.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":6,"initial":"d"},{"id":301,"targetId":301,"type":"tc","text":"The auditor initially assumed that the two totals matched; discovering a {{1}} of several million dollars forced her to reopen the investigation.","choices":[["discrepancy","convergence","surplus","consensus","correlation"]],"answers":[["discrepancy"]],"explanation":"The totals fail to agree, producing a discrepancy. Surplus would require knowing that one amount exceeded a need or balance, while the sentence establishes only disagreement.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":7,"initial":"d"},{"id":302,"targetId":302,"type":"tc","text":"The catalog treats the manuscripts as {{1}} works rather than chapters of a single composition, although their {{2}} arrangement makes their historical relationship easy to follow.","choices":[["discrete","interchangeable","inseparable"],["chronological","haphazard","arbitrary"]],"answers":[["discrete"],["chronological"]],"explanation":"Discrete means separate and distinct. Ordering by date is chronological and helps readers trace historical relationships without implying that the manuscripts form one work.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"chronological","meaning":"arranged in order of time"}],"section":7,"initial":"d"},{"id":303,"targetId":303,"type":"se","text":"Her refusal even to inspect the amateur’s results revealed {{1}} for his work, an attitude of superiority that no examination of the evidence had earned.","choices":[["disdain","contempt","solicitude","curiosity","reverence","gratitude"]],"answers":[["disdain","contempt"]],"explanation":"Refusal based on assumed inferiority is disdain or contempt. Concern, curiosity, respect, and gratitude cannot explain the dismissive attitude.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":7,"initial":"d"},{"id":304,"targetId":304,"type":"tc","text":"Before assessing the effect of the new policy, the economist had to {{1}} it from the simultaneous changes in taxation and employment.","choices":[["disentangle","conflate","derive","conceal","amalgamate"]],"answers":[["disentangle"]],"explanation":"Separating overlapping influences is disentangling them. Conflate and amalgamate combine things; derive and conceal do not isolate a causal effect.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":7,"initial":"d"},{"id":305,"targetId":305,"type":"tc","text":"The spokesman’s claim of complete ignorance was {{1}}: minutes proved he had attended every meeting, making his apparent innocence an exercise in {{2}}.","choices":[["disingenuous","ingenuous","fortuitous"],["deception","candor","detachment"]],"answers":[["disingenuous"],["deception"]],"explanation":"Pretending not to know what one demonstrably knows is disingenuous. The minutes expose deliberate deception rather than honesty or impartiality.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"deception","meaning":"deliberate misleading of others"}],"section":7,"initial":"d"},{"id":306,"targetId":306,"type":"tc","text":"To obtain a genuinely {{1}} assessment, the foundation excluded reviewers who stood to gain financially from either approval or rejection of the project.","choices":[["disinterested","indifferent","partisan","perfunctory","enthusiastic"]],"answers":[["disinterested"]],"explanation":"Removing personal stakes promotes impartiality, the meaning of disinterested. Indifferent means unconcerned, which is not the desired quality in an attentive reviewer.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":7,"initial":"d"},{"id":307,"targetId":307,"type":"tc","text":"The exhibition brought together {{1}} objects, from navigational instruments to household textiles; rather than impose false {{2}}, the curator offered a detailed {{3}} of the different networks that connected them.","choices":[["disparate","identical","interchangeable"],["uniformity","variety","complexity"],["exposition","evasion","obfuscation"]],"answers":[["disparate"],["uniformity"],["exposition"]],"explanation":"The objects are fundamentally different, or disparate. The curator avoids pretending they are uniform and explains their real connections through an exposition, not evasion or obscurity.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"uniformity","meaning":"sameness throughout"},{"word":"exposition","meaning":"a developed explanation"}],"section":7,"initial":"d"},{"id":308,"targetId":308,"type":"se","text":"Though the dispute involved her closest friends, the mediator remained {{1}}, weighing each account without allowing affection or anger to determine her conclusions.","choices":[["dispassionate","impartial","vindictive","fervent","agitated","credulous"]],"answers":[["dispassionate","impartial"]],"explanation":"Unbiased, emotionally controlled assessment is dispassionate or impartial. Fervor and agitation emphasize emotion; vindictiveness and credulity distort judgment.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":7,"initial":"d"},{"id":309,"targetId":309,"type":"tc","text":"The emergency charter allowed the council to {{1}} with ordinary procurement rules, but the exception did not confer unlimited {{2}} to spend public funds without oversight.","choices":[["dispense","comply","interfere"],["capacity","reluctance","inability"]],"answers":[["dispense"],["capacity"]],"explanation":"Dispense with means do without. The exception grants some ability, or capacity, to act outside normal rules, but not unrestricted power.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"capacity","meaning":"ability or power to act"}],"section":7,"initial":"d"},{"id":310,"targetId":310,"type":"tc","text":"The reserve’s barriers were designed to prevent pollutants from entering the river, not merely to {{1}} them across a wider stretch of water.","choices":[["disperse","neutralize","capture","identify","eradicate"]],"answers":[["disperse"]],"explanation":"Spreading pollutants more widely is dispersal, which differs from preventing pollution. Neutralizing or eradicating them would remove their harmful presence rather than redistribute it.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":7,"initial":"d"},{"id":311,"targetId":311,"type":"tc","text":"Certain that revealing her disappointment would weaken her bargaining position, she chose to {{1}}, greeting the unfavorable offer with an expression of satisfaction.","choices":[["dissemble","protest","confess","capitulate","lament"]],"answers":[["dissemble"]],"explanation":"Displaying satisfaction while concealing disappointment is dissembling. Protest, confession, and lament would reveal her reaction; capitulation concerns accepting terms rather than masking feelings.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":7,"initial":"d"},{"id":312,"targetId":312,"type":"tc","text":"The institute’s original purpose was to {{1}} technical knowledge, but an increasingly {{2}} publication policy made its findings available only to a tiny circle of subscribers.","choices":[["disseminate","withhold","discredit"],["exclusionary","inclusive","indiscriminate"]],"answers":[["disseminate"],["exclusionary"]],"explanation":"Disseminate means spread widely. Restricting access to a select few is exclusionary, contradicting the original educational aim.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"exclusionary","meaning":"serving to keep people out"}],"section":7,"initial":"d"},{"id":313,"targetId":313,"type":"se","text":"Even the committee’s most loyal member felt obliged to {{1}} when the final recommendation contradicted the evidence she had collected.","choices":[["dissent","disagree","acquiesce","capitulate","celebrate","equivocate"]],"answers":[["dissent","disagree"]],"explanation":"The conflict with her evidence prompts opposition: dissent or disagree. Acquiescence and capitulation accept the recommendation, while equivocation avoids taking the clear position described.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"disagree","meaning":"hold or express a contrary view"}],"section":7,"initial":"d"},{"id":314,"targetId":314,"type":"tc","text":"Once celebrated as an architect of official policy, the economist became a prominent {{1}} after publicly rejecting its central assumptions.","choices":[["dissident","apologist","beneficiary","successor","intermediary"]],"answers":[["dissident"]],"explanation":"Open opposition to an official position makes someone a dissident. An apologist defends it; the other roles do not necessarily entail opposition.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":7,"initial":"d"},{"id":315,"targetId":315,"type":"tc","text":"The memoir’s repeated assurances of humility create a curious {{1}} with its pages of self-praise; the two elements are difficult to {{2}}.","choices":[["dissonance","harmony","continuity"],["reconcile","separate","detect"]],"answers":[["dissonance"],["reconcile"]],"explanation":"Claims of humility clash with self-praise, producing dissonance. Reconciling the conflicting elements is difficult; their presence and separateness are already clear.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[],"section":7,"initial":"d"},{"id":316,"targetId":316,"type":"tc","text":"Engineers left space around the sealed pouch because heat could cause its contents to expand and {{1}} the flexible covering.","choices":[["distend","perforate","desiccate","compress","dissolve"]],"answers":[["distend"]],"explanation":"Expanding contents stretch the covering outward, or distend it. Perforation is puncturing and compression is the opposite; drying or dissolving is not described.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":7,"initial":"d"},{"id":317,"targetId":317,"type":"tc","text":"The editor had to {{1}} the witness’s rambling account into a few paragraphs, preserving its {{2}} while removing repetitions; the final summary’s {{3}} made its central claim unmistakable.","choices":[["distill","inflate","fragment"],["essence","digressions","verbosity"],["economy","redundancy","opacity"]],"answers":[["distill"],["essence"],["economy"]],"explanation":"Distill means extract the essentials. Preserving the essence while removing repetition produces economy of expression, rather than wordiness or obscurity.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"essence","meaning":"the central or indispensable content"}],"section":7,"initial":"d"},{"id":318,"targetId":318,"type":"se","text":"The cropped photograph would {{1}} the event by making a nearly empty hall appear crowded.","choices":[["distort","misrepresent","corroborate","commemorate","clarify","authenticate"]],"answers":[["distort","misrepresent"]],"explanation":"Creating a misleading impression is both distorting and misrepresenting. The other choices imply confirmation, remembrance, or accurate clarification.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"misrepresent","meaning":"present inaccurately or misleadingly"}],"section":7,"initial":"d"},{"id":319,"targetId":319,"type":"tc","text":"The normally composed archivist appeared {{1}} on discovering the flooded storeroom; her {{2}} response contrasted sharply with her usual calm.","choices":[["distressed","elated","complacent"],["agitated","equable","insouciant"]],"answers":[["distressed"],["agitated"]],"explanation":"The damage causes distress, not joy or satisfaction. Agitation contrasts with composure; equable and insouciant would imply calm or unconcern.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"agitated","meaning":"troubled and visibly unsettled"}],"section":7,"initial":"d"},{"id":320,"targetId":320,"type":"tc","text":"Although the researchers began with the same assumptions, their conclusions became increasingly {{1}} as each incorporated a different body of evidence.","choices":[["divergent","identical","convergent","interchangeable","unanimous"]],"answers":[["divergent"]],"explanation":"Different evidence sends the conclusions apart, making them divergent. The alternatives describe agreement, similarity, or movement together.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":7,"initial":"d"},{"id":321,"targetId":321,"type":"tc","text":"The critic objected that the theory was entirely {{1}} from observable conditions: no conceivable evidence could either confirm or disprove it.","choices":[["divorced","derived","indistinguishable","inseparable","assembled"]],"answers":[["divorced"]],"explanation":"A theory insulated from all observation is disconnected, or divorced, from observable conditions. Derived, inseparable, and indistinguishable suggest close connection instead.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":7,"initial":"d"},{"id":322,"targetId":322,"type":"tc","text":"The negotiator promised not to {{1}} the confidential terms; maintaining that trust required {{2}} even when reporters offered flattering accounts in exchange for details.","choices":[["divulge","conceal","honor"],["discretion","candor","loquacity"]],"answers":[["divulge"],["discretion"]],"explanation":"Divulge means reveal private information. Protecting confidentiality requires discretion rather than openness or excessive talkativeness.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"discretion","meaning":"care in protecting sensitive information"}],"section":7,"initial":"d"},{"id":323,"targetId":323,"type":"se","text":"The minister’s approach was {{1}}: she applied the same rigid formula in every province, refusing to modify it even when local evidence showed it failed.","choices":[["doctrinaire","dogmatic","pragmatic","experimental","tentative","eclectic"]],"answers":[["doctrinaire","dogmatic"]],"explanation":"Rigid adherence to a formula despite evidence is doctrinaire or dogmatic. Pragmatic and experimental approaches respond to results; tentative and eclectic ones allow alternatives.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":7,"initial":"d"},{"id":324,"targetId":324,"type":"tc","text":"The historian gave greater weight to {{1}} evidence such as signed contracts and dated receipts than to anecdotes recalled decades later.","choices":[["documentary","circumstantial","hypothetical","intuitive","mythic"]],"answers":[["documentary"]],"explanation":"Contracts and receipts are written records, hence documentary evidence. The contrast is with recollected stories, not with indirect evidence in general.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":7,"initial":"d"},{"id":325,"targetId":325,"type":"tc","text":"The investigation succeeded through {{1}} persistence rather than sudden inspiration; even its least glamorous tasks received {{2}} attention.","choices":[["dogged","sporadic","desultory"],["assiduous","cursory","capricious"]],"answers":[["dogged"],["assiduous"]],"explanation":"Dogged describes sustained determination. Assiduous attention is diligent and consistent, unlike superficial or changeable effort.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"assiduous","meaning":"persistently diligent"}],"section":7,"initial":"d"},{"id":326,"targetId":326,"type":"tc","text":"The professor welcomed attempts to refute her claims, reasoning that a theory becomes {{1}} when its defenders treat challenges as forbidden rather than potentially informative.","choices":[["dogmatic","provisional","empirical","falsifiable","tentative"]],"answers":[["dogmatic"]],"explanation":"Refusing all challenge turns a theory into unquestionable doctrine, or makes it dogmatic. Provisional, falsifiable, and tentative theories remain open to revision.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":7,"initial":"d"},{"id":327,"targetId":327,"type":"tc","text":"The renovators dismissed the lobby as {{1}}, confusing unfashionable decoration with structural {{2}}; an engineer found the building sound, exposing the premise of demolition as {{3}}.","choices":[["dowdy","ostentatious","resplendent"],["decay","integrity","stability"],["erroneous","compelling","incontestable"]],"answers":[["dowdy"],["decay"],["erroneous"]],"explanation":"Dowdy means dull and unfashionable. That appearance was mistaken for structural decay; the engineer’s finding makes the demolition premise erroneous, or wrong.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"decay","meaning":"progressive deterioration"},{"word":"erroneous","meaning":"mistaken or incorrect"}],"section":7,"initial":"d"},{"id":328,"targetId":328,"type":"se","text":"Her {{1}} account of the committee meeting made listeners laugh precisely because she described its absurdities with such a straight face.","choices":[["droll","wry","solemn","vitriolic","tearful","pedantic"]],"answers":[["droll","wry"]],"explanation":"Understated amusement at absurdity is droll or wry. A straight-faced delivery can carry humor; it does not make the content solemn, bitter, tearful, or obsessively instructional.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"wry","meaning":"dryly or ironically humorous"}],"section":7,"initial":"d"},{"id":329,"targetId":329,"type":"tc","text":"The restoration’s authenticity was {{1}} because no records survived of the original colors; the confident publicity therefore deserved less {{2}} than it received.","choices":[["dubious","unassailable","indisputable"],["credence","scrutiny","skepticism"]],"answers":[["dubious"],["credence"]],"explanation":"Missing evidence makes authenticity questionable, or dubious. The unsupported publicity deserves less belief, or credence, not less examination or doubt.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"credence","meaning":"belief or acceptance as true"}],"section":7,"initial":"d"},{"id":330,"targetId":330,"type":"tc","text":"By showing investors fabricated contracts, the promoter hoped to {{1}} them into funding a business that did not exist.","choices":[["dupe","dissuade","reassure","compensate","vindicate"]],"answers":[["dupe"]],"explanation":"Fabricated evidence used to obtain money constitutes deception: dupe fits. Reassure does not itself mean deceive, and the other verbs do not describe the fraud.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":7,"initial":"d"},{"id":331,"targetId":331,"type":"tc","text":"The envoy was {{1}}, privately promising each rival exclusive support while publicly insisting that he had made no commitments.","choices":[["duplicitous","ingenuous","impartial","reticent","scrupulous"]],"answers":[["duplicitous"]],"explanation":"Conflicting secret promises and public denial establish deliberate two-faced deception. Duplicitous fits; reticence merely means reserve and does not capture lying.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":7,"initial":"d"},{"id":332,"targetId":332,"type":"tc","text":"The signature was obtained under {{1}}, not through free agreement; the threatened confiscation made claims of voluntary {{2}} untenable.","choices":[["duress","consensus","reconciliation"],["consent","protest","resistance"]],"answers":[["duress"],["consent"]],"explanation":"A threat forcing agreement is duress. Such coercion invalidates claims of voluntary consent, rather than claims of protest or resistance.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"consent","meaning":"agreement or permission"}],"section":7,"initial":"d"},{"id":333,"targetId":333,"type":"se","text":"The expedition’s {{1}} reserves forced daily ration cuts as food supplies grew smaller with every mile traveled.","choices":[["dwindling","diminishing","abundant","renewable","inexhaustible","superfluous"]],"answers":[["dwindling","diminishing"]],"explanation":"Steadily shrinking reserves are dwindling or diminishing. The other descriptions would not explain ration cuts caused by depletion.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"diminishing","meaning":"becoming smaller or less"}],"section":7,"initial":"d"},{"id":334,"targetId":334,"type":"tc","text":"The usually restrained pianist was positively {{1}} after the successful premiere, embracing colleagues and speaking excitedly about new projects.","choices":[["ebullient","despondent","phlegmatic","diffident","morose"]],"answers":[["ebullient"]],"explanation":"Overflowing enthusiasm makes the pianist ebullient. The alternatives indicate sadness, calm, low confidence, or gloom.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":7,"initial":"e"},{"id":335,"targetId":335,"type":"tc","text":"The inventor’s habits seemed {{1}}, but the historian warned against treating unusual routines as a {{2}} of scientific creativity: many unremarkable workers kept similarly odd schedules.","choices":[["eccentric","conventional","uniform"],["determinant","digression","consequence"]],"answers":[["eccentric"],["determinant"]],"explanation":"Unusual habits are eccentric. Their presence among ordinary workers shows that they need not determine, or causally produce, creativity.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"determinant","meaning":"a factor influencing an outcome"}],"section":7,"initial":"e"},{"id":336,"targetId":336,"type":"tc","text":"The reform promoted officers from the lowest {{1}} of the hierarchy, giving junior personnel access to responsibilities once reserved for senior commanders.","choices":[["echelon","trajectory","interval","coalition","frontier"]],"answers":[["echelon"]],"explanation":"Echelon means a rank or level within an organization. Lowest and hierarchy directly identify the intended sense.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":7,"initial":"e"},{"id":337,"targetId":337,"type":"tc","text":"The anthology is deliberately {{1}}, drawing from rival schools rather than a single {{2}}; its diversity reflects a refusal to let {{3}} prescribe every editorial choice.","choices":[["eclectic","homogeneous","parochial"],["tradition","controversy","experiment"],["dogma","curiosity","pluralism"]],"answers":[["eclectic"],["tradition"],["dogma"]],"explanation":"Eclectic selections come from varied sources rather than one tradition. Dogma is a rigid body of beliefs; refusing it allows editorial diversity.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"tradition","meaning":"an established inherited practice"},{"word":"dogma","meaning":"doctrine treated as beyond question"}],"section":7,"initial":"e"},{"id":338,"targetId":338,"type":"se","text":"The unexpected discovery threatened to {{1}} the team’s earlier achievement, attracting nearly all the attention that had once belonged to the older work.","choices":[["eclipse","overshadow","vindicate","replicate","invalidate","authenticate"]],"answers":[["eclipse","overshadow"]],"explanation":"Diverting attention away from an earlier achievement is eclipsing or overshadowing it. The new discovery need not disprove or reproduce the earlier result.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"overshadow","meaning":"draw attention away from"}],"section":7,"initial":"e"},{"id":339,"targetId":339,"type":"tc","text":"Her travel plan was {{1}} without being austere: careful scheduling reduced unnecessary {{2}} while preserving the visits most important to the group.","choices":[["economical","profligate","improvident"],["expenditure","leisure","accuracy"]],"answers":[["economical"],["expenditure"]],"explanation":"Economical planning uses money carefully. Eliminating unnecessary expenditure saves resources without requiring deprivation; reducing accuracy is irrelevant and reducing leisure is not established.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"expenditure","meaning":"money or resources spent"}],"section":7,"initial":"e"},{"id":340,"targetId":340,"type":"tc","text":"The essay’s power derives from its {{1}} of expression: every sentence advances the argument, and none repeats work already done.","choices":[["economy","prolixity","ambiguity","ornamentation","redundancy"]],"answers":[["economy"]],"explanation":"Efficient language with no wasted or repeated sentences displays economy of expression. Prolixity and redundancy are the opposite.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":7,"initial":"e"},{"id":341,"targetId":341,"type":"tc","text":"The museum hoped its workshops would {{1}} visitors, deepening their understanding of how evidence is assessed rather than merely entertaining them.","choices":[["edify","beguile","intimidate","flatter","polarize"]],"answers":[["edify"]],"explanation":"Deepening intellectual understanding is edification. The aim goes beyond entertainment or flattery and does not involve frightening or dividing visitors.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":7,"initial":"e"},{"id":342,"targetId":342,"type":"tc","text":"The scandal proved unexpectedly {{1}} for the profession: by exposing weaknesses in peer review, it helped researchers {{2}} safeguards they had previously taken for granted.","choices":[["edifying","inconsequential","inscrutable"],["enhance","dismantle","evade"]],"answers":[["edifying"],["enhance"]],"explanation":"The scandal teaches a useful lesson, making it edifying. Researchers improve, or enhance, safeguards in response; dismantling or evading them would undermine the lesson.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"enhance","meaning":"improve or increase"}],"section":7,"initial":"e"},{"id":343,"targetId":343,"type":"se","text":"A remedy is {{1}} only if it produces the intended improvement; a persuasive explanation of how it ought to work is not enough.","choices":[["efficacious","effective","plausible","fashionable","inexpensive","traditional"]],"answers":[["efficacious","effective"]],"explanation":"Actual success makes a remedy efficacious or effective. Plausibility, popularity, price, and tradition do not establish that it works.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"effective","meaning":"producing the intended result"}],"section":7,"initial":"e"},{"id":344,"targetId":344,"type":"tc","text":"The report’s errors were not minor slips but {{1}} distortions, including fabricated quotations and statistics altered to reverse their meaning.","choices":[["egregious","inadvertent","subtle","trivial","innocuous"]],"answers":[["egregious"]],"explanation":"Fabrication and reversed statistics are exceptionally serious, or egregious. They cannot reasonably be characterized as harmless, trivial, or subtle.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":7,"initial":"e"},{"id":345,"targetId":345,"type":"tc","text":"The normally measured scientist was {{1}} by the replication results, yet her public statement remained {{2}}, acknowledging both the success and the experiment’s limitations.","choices":[["elated","crestfallen","indifferent"],["modest","extravagant","triumphal"]],"answers":[["elated"],["modest"]],"explanation":"Successful replication makes her extremely happy, or elated. A restrained statement acknowledging limits is modest, rather than extravagant or triumphalist.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[],"section":7,"initial":"e"},{"id":346,"targetId":346,"type":"tc","text":"Although its title promised advanced analysis, the book never moved beyond {{1}} principles familiar to anyone who had completed an introductory course.","choices":[["elementary","esoteric","innovative","controversial","recondite"]],"answers":[["elementary"]],"explanation":"Introductory principles are elementary. Esoteric and recondite imply specialized difficulty, while innovation and controversy are not the stated shortcoming.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":7,"initial":"e"},{"id":347,"targetId":347,"type":"tc","text":"The interviewer used open questions to {{1}} detailed memories, taking care not to {{2}} answers; suggestive prompts could otherwise turn recollection into an {{3}} shaped by the interviewer’s expectations.","choices":[["elicit","suppress","contradict"],["dictate","record","compare"],["artifice","corroboration","observation"]],"answers":[["elicit"],["dictate"],["artifice"]],"explanation":"Elicit means draw out responses. Dictating them would impose content, creating an artifice, or constructed deception, instead of reliable recollection; merely recording or comparing answers does not impose them.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"artifice","meaning":"a contrived deceptive device"}],"section":7,"initial":"e"},{"id":348,"targetId":348,"type":"se","text":"The academy’s {{1}} admission policy favored a small privileged circle, treating inherited social position as evidence of intellectual superiority.","choices":[["elitist","exclusionary","egalitarian","inclusive","indiscriminate","meritocratic"]],"answers":[["elitist","exclusionary"]],"explanation":"Preference for a privileged group makes the policy elitist and exclusionary. Egalitarian, inclusive, indiscriminate, and meritocratic policies would not privilege inherited position in this way.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"exclusionary","meaning":"serving to keep people out"}],"section":7,"initial":"e"},{"id":349,"targetId":349,"type":"tc","text":"Though the advocate was {{1}}, the panel refused to mistake persuasive delivery for evidentiary {{2}}; elegant phrasing could not repair unsupported premises.","choices":[["eloquent","inarticulate","taciturn"],["sufficiency","deficiency","scarcity"]],"answers":[["eloquent"],["sufficiency"]],"explanation":"Eloquent describes fluent persuasive expression. The panel demands sufficient evidence, which rhetorical skill cannot supply; deficiency and scarcity are the very problems present.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"sufficiency","meaning":"adequacy for a purpose"}],"section":7,"initial":"e"},{"id":350,"targetId":350,"type":"tc","text":"The instructor used a concrete example to {{1}} a distinction that the abstract definition had left obscure.","choices":[["elucidate","complicate","invalidate","disguise","exacerbate"]],"answers":[["elucidate"]],"explanation":"Elucidate means make clear through explanation. The concrete example clarifies the obscure distinction rather than worsening or concealing it.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":7,"initial":"e"},{"id":351,"targetId":351,"type":"tc","text":"The editor would not permit the memoirist to {{1}} the episode with invented dialogue, however much more dramatic the additions made it.","choices":[["embellish","verify","condense","excise","authenticate"]],"answers":[["embellish"]],"explanation":"Invented decorative details embellish an account. The objection concerns adding fiction, not checking, shortening, deleting, or authenticating the episode.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":8,"initial":"e"},{"id":352,"targetId":352,"type":"tc","text":"The panel included several {{1}} scholars, but their reputations did not make their judgment {{2}}; even celebrated authorities could overlook contradictory evidence.","choices":[["eminent","obscure","discredited"],["infallible","fallible","tentative"]],"answers":[["eminent"],["infallible"]],"explanation":"Distinguished scholars are eminent. Reputation does not guarantee freedom from error, or infallibility; the second sentence explains that limit.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"infallible","meaning":"incapable of error"}],"section":8,"initial":"e"},{"id":353,"targetId":353,"type":"se","text":"The theory remained speculative until the team supplied {{1}} support through repeated measurements in the field.","choices":[["empirical","observational","anecdotal","conjectural","metaphysical","dogmatic"]],"answers":[["empirical","observational"]],"explanation":"Support derived from measurements is empirical and observational. Speculation, abstract doctrine, and isolated anecdotes do not express the systematic field evidence described.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"observational","meaning":"derived from watching or measuring"}],"section":8,"initial":"e"},{"id":354,"targetId":354,"type":"tc","text":"The apprentice hoped to {{1}} her mentor’s achievements, studying his methods so that she might eventually equal his skill.","choices":[["emulate","disparage","conceal","catalog","forfeit"]],"answers":[["emulate"]],"explanation":"Emulate means imitate with the aim of matching or surpassing. Studying methods to equal skill is more than recording or hiding achievements.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":8,"initial":"e"},{"id":355,"targetId":355,"type":"tc","text":"The installation’s shifting colors could {{1}} visitors, but its appeal was not merely visual: an accompanying essay offered a lucid {{2}} of the mathematics behind the patterns.","choices":[["enchant","repel","exasperate"],["exposition","evasion","contradiction"]],"answers":[["enchant"],["exposition"]],"explanation":"The colors delight, or enchant, viewers. A lucid exposition explains the underlying mathematics; evasion and contradiction would not provide understanding.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"exposition","meaning":"a developed explanation"}],"section":8,"initial":"e"},{"id":356,"targetId":356,"type":"tc","text":"The scholar’s knowledge was {{1}}, encompassing subjects as far apart as ancient astronomy and modern textile production.","choices":[["encyclopedic","parochial","fragmentary","rudimentary","specialized"]],"answers":[["encyclopedic"]],"explanation":"Broad, comprehensive knowledge across many domains is encyclopedic. Specialized, parochial, and fragmentary knowledge is narrower or incomplete.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":8,"initial":"e"},{"id":357,"targetId":357,"type":"tc","text":"The disease was {{1}} to the region, recurring there regularly rather than arriving as a single {{2}} outbreak; that persistence made a temporary response {{3}}.","choices":[["endemic","extraneous","alien"],["isolated","perennial","recurrent"],["inadequate","sufficient","optimal"]],"answers":[["endemic"],["isolated"],["inadequate"]],"explanation":"Endemic describes regular local occurrence. An isolated outbreak is the contrasting one-time event; sustained recurrence makes a merely temporary response inadequate.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"isolated","meaning":"separate and not part of recurrence"},{"word":"inadequate","meaning":"insufficient for the purpose"}],"section":8,"initial":"e"},{"id":358,"targetId":358,"type":"se","text":"Instead of restoring her concentration, the prolonged heat seemed to {{1}} her, leaving her too exhausted to finish even simple calculations.","choices":[["enervate","debilitate","galvanize","invigorate","stimulate","fortify"]],"answers":[["enervate","debilitate"]],"explanation":"The heat drains strength, so enervate and debilitate fit. The other verbs imply increased energy or strength, contrary to exhaustion.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"debilitate","meaning":"weaken or drain strength"}],"section":8,"initial":"e"},{"id":359,"targetId":359,"type":"tc","text":"The director hoped that candid discussion would {{1}} trust, but her refusal to answer questions instead gave her assurances an air of {{2}}.","choices":[["engender","erode","preclude"],["insincerity","probity","candor"]],"answers":[["engender"],["insincerity"]],"explanation":"Engender means bring into existence. Evading questions undermines candid discussion and suggests insincerity, not honesty.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"insincerity","meaning":"lack of honest intent or feeling"}],"section":8,"initial":"e"},{"id":360,"targetId":360,"type":"tc","text":"The missing interval in the expedition’s diary remained an {{1}}: neither the surviving crew nor the physical evidence explained what had occurred.","choices":[["enigma","axiom","certainty","anachronism","consensus"]],"answers":[["enigma"]],"explanation":"An unexplained, puzzling matter is an enigma. Anachronism concerns something in the wrong time period, not a gap that resists explanation.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":8,"initial":"e"},{"id":361,"targetId":361,"type":"tc","text":"Her final letter was {{1}}, offering hints that supported several incompatible readings while resolving none of them.","choices":[["enigmatic","unequivocal","prosaic","transparent","redundant"]],"answers":[["enigmatic"]],"explanation":"Multiple unresolved interpretations make the letter puzzling, or enigmatic. Transparent and unequivocal would describe clarity; repetition alone does not explain the mystery.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":8,"initial":"e"},{"id":362,"targetId":362,"type":"tc","text":"The rivals’ polite public exchanges concealed deep {{1}}, making the apparent reconciliation a matter of diplomacy rather than genuine {{2}}.","choices":[["enmity","affinity","solidarity"],["amity","hostility","rancor"]],"answers":[["enmity"],["amity"]],"explanation":"Enmity is profound hostility. Genuine amity would mean friendship, which the polite diplomatic facade does not establish.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"amity","meaning":"friendly relations"}],"section":8,"initial":"e"},{"id":363,"targetId":363,"type":"se","text":"Endless repetitions of the same social rituals produced {{1}} in the visitor, who longed for anything that might interrupt the monotony.","choices":[["ennui","tedium","exhilaration","trepidation","rapture","indignation"]],"answers":[["ennui","tedium"]],"explanation":"Weariness from monotonous repetition is ennui or tedium. Excitement, fear, delight, and anger do not match the specified boredom.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"tedium","meaning":"weariness caused by monotony"}],"section":8,"initial":"e"},{"id":364,"targetId":364,"type":"tc","text":"Even listeners unfamiliar with the subject found that the lecturer could {{1}} them, holding their attention through the entire demanding presentation.","choices":[["enthrall","alienate","intimidate","bemuse","reprove"]],"answers":[["enthrall"]],"explanation":"To enthrall is to captivate completely. The clue is sustained attention, not discomfort, confusion, or criticism.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":8,"initial":"e"},{"id":365,"targetId":365,"type":"tc","text":"The sponsor behaved as though she were {{1}} to control the research merely because she supplied the funds; the scientists rejected that presumed {{2}}.","choices":[["entitled","indifferent","averse"],["entitlement","incompetence","reluctance"]],"answers":[["entitled"],["entitlement"]],"explanation":"Entitled means possessing or assuming a right. The scientists reject her presumed entitlement, or claim to authority, rather than her ability or willingness.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"entitlement","meaning":"a right or presumed claim"}],"section":8,"initial":"e"},{"id":366,"targetId":366,"type":"tc","text":"The practice was so {{1}} that even administrators who opposed it reproduced it automatically in their own departments.","choices":[["entrenched","ephemeral","nascent","optional","aberrant"]],"answers":[["entrenched"]],"explanation":"A practice reproduced despite opposition is deeply established and hard to change: entrenched. Nascent and ephemeral would suggest something new or short-lived.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":8,"initial":"e"},{"id":367,"targetId":367,"type":"tc","text":"The exhibit’s online popularity was {{1}}, disappearing within days; the museum’s {{2}} visitor numbers then exposed the error of treating a brief surge as {{3}} growth.","choices":[["ephemeral","perennial","inexhaustible"],["dwindling","burgeoning","stable"],["sustained","temporary","illusory"]],"answers":[["ephemeral"],["dwindling"],["sustained"]],"explanation":"Ephemeral means short-lived. Falling, or dwindling, attendance contradicts an expectation of sustained growth; the surge itself was real but brief.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"sustained","meaning":"continuing over time"}],"section":8,"initial":"e"},{"id":368,"targetId":368,"type":"se","text":"She received both the lavish praise and the furious criticism with {{1}}, maintaining the same composure under either pressure.","choices":[["equanimity","composure","elation","resentment","alarm","exasperation"]],"answers":[["equanimity","composure"]],"explanation":"Calm emotional balance is equanimity or composure. The unchanged response to opposite pressures excludes excitement, anger, fear, and irritation.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"composure","meaning":"calm self-control"}],"section":8,"initial":"e"},{"id":369,"targetId":369,"type":"tc","text":"An {{1}} distribution need not give every district an identical sum; taking unequal needs into account may be necessary to {{2}} fairness.","choices":[["equitable","arbitrary","capricious"],["enhance","subvert","obscure"]],"answers":[["equitable"],["enhance"]],"explanation":"Equitable means fair, not necessarily numerically equal. Adjusting for different needs can enhance, or improve, fairness rather than undermine it.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"enhance","meaning":"improve or increase"}],"section":8,"initial":"e"},{"id":370,"targetId":370,"type":"tc","text":"The minister’s answer was deliberately {{1}}: both supporters and opponents of the proposal could quote it as evidence that she agreed with them.","choices":[["equivocal","categorical","forthright","irrevocable","unanimous"]],"answers":[["equivocal"]],"explanation":"An answer supporting incompatible interpretations is equivocal. Categorical and forthright statements would leave less room for conflicting claims.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":8,"initial":"e"},{"id":371,"targetId":371,"type":"tc","text":"Pressed for a clear yes or no, the witness continued to {{1}}, offering carefully balanced phrases that committed him to neither position.","choices":[["equivocate","capitulate","testify","recant","assent"]],"answers":[["equivocate"]],"explanation":"Equivocate means use ambiguity to avoid commitment. Testify merely means give evidence and does not specifically describe the evasive phrasing.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":8,"initial":"e"},{"id":372,"targetId":372,"type":"tc","text":"The program sought to {{1}} the invasive plant, but it achieved only containment; claims of complete success confused limited {{2}} with total elimination.","choices":[["eradicate","cultivate","propagate"],["efficacy","futility","negligence"]],"answers":[["eradicate"],["efficacy"]],"explanation":"Eradicate means remove completely. Containment demonstrates some efficacy, or effectiveness, but falls short of eradication; it is not complete futility.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"efficacy","meaning":"ability to achieve a result"}],"section":8,"initial":"e"},{"id":373,"targetId":373,"type":"se","text":"The sensor’s {{1}} readings jumped unpredictably even when the laboratory conditions remained constant.","choices":[["erratic","capricious","uniform","methodical","reproducible","steady"]],"answers":[["erratic","capricious"]],"explanation":"Unpredictable variation is erratic or capricious. Constant external conditions make the instability especially evident; the other choices suggest consistency.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":8,"initial":"e"},{"id":374,"targetId":374,"type":"tc","text":"The essay was undeniably {{1}}, drawing with precision on several ancient languages, yet its learning never displaced a clear central argument.","choices":[["erudite","jejune","illiterate","provincial","superficial"]],"answers":[["erudite"]],"explanation":"Precise use of several ancient languages displays extensive scholarly learning, or erudition. The sentence rejects the idea that such learning necessarily obscures argument.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":8,"initial":"e"},{"id":375,"targetId":375,"type":"tc","text":"The mediator feared that public accusations would {{1}} the dispute, so she acted as an {{2}}, carrying proposals privately between the two sides.","choices":[["escalate","resolve","defuse"],["intermediary","antagonist","partisan"]],"answers":[["escalate"],["intermediary"]],"explanation":"Accusations risk intensifying, or escalating, conflict. A person conveying proposals between sides is an intermediary, not necessarily a combatant or partisan.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"intermediary","meaning":"a go-between"}],"section":8,"initial":"e"},{"id":376,"targetId":376,"type":"tc","text":"The architect chose to {{1}} fashionable embellishments, preferring materials and forms whose value did not depend on current taste.","choices":[["eschew","accumulate","emulate","advertise","exalt"]],"answers":[["eschew"]],"explanation":"Deliberately avoiding embellishments is eschewing them. The alternatives would acquire, imitate, promote, or praise the fashions she rejects.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":8,"initial":"e"},{"id":377,"targetId":377,"type":"tc","text":"The notation seemed {{1}} to newcomers, although experts found it {{2}}; the tutor therefore had to {{3}} conventions that specialists normally left unstated.","choices":[["esoteric","elementary","ubiquitous"],["intuitive","inscrutable","incoherent"],["explicate","suppress","invalidate"]],"answers":[["esoteric"],["intuitive"],["explicate"]],"explanation":"Specialized knowledge makes the notation esoteric to outsiders but intuitive to experts. The tutor must explicate, or explain, tacit conventions rather than hide or invalidate them.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"intuitive","meaning":"readily understood without conscious analysis"},{"word":"explicate","meaning":"explain carefully"}],"section":8,"initial":"e"},{"id":378,"targetId":378,"type":"se","text":"The association refused to {{1}} the doctrine until its members had examined the evidence supporting it.","choices":[["espouse","endorse","refute","discredit","conceal","misconstrue"]],"answers":[["espouse","endorse"]],"explanation":"Espouse and endorse both mean support a doctrine. The association withholds support pending evidence; it is not necessarily rejecting, hiding, or misunderstanding the doctrine.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"endorse","meaning":"express support or approval"}],"section":8,"initial":"e"},{"id":379,"targetId":379,"type":"tc","text":"Even the researcher’s opponents regarded her intellectual honesty as {{1}}; they could reject her conclusions while continuing to {{2}} her candor.","choices":[["estimable","venal","contemptible"],["laud","denigrate","mistrust"]],"answers":[["estimable"],["laud"]],"explanation":"Estimable means deserving respect. Laud means praise, which fits acknowledging honest conduct while disagreeing about conclusions.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"laud","meaning":"praise highly"}],"section":8,"initial":"e"},{"id":380,"targetId":380,"type":"tc","text":"Once close collaborators, the artists became {{1}} after the dispute and refused even to attend exhibitions in which the other participated.","choices":[["estranged","reconciled","interdependent","congenial","indebted"]],"answers":[["estranged"]],"explanation":"Lost personal closeness and active avoidance indicate estrangement. Reconciled and congenial suggest restored friendship, while indebtedness does not explain avoidance.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":8,"initial":"e"},{"id":381,"targetId":381,"type":"tc","text":"The speaker’s {{1}} emphasized the scientist’s generosity and intellectual courage, allowing mourners to remember her life through praise rather than through a list of dates.","choices":[["eulogy","indictment","chronology","retraction","diatribe"]],"answers":[["eulogy"]],"explanation":"A speech praising a deceased person is a eulogy. Chronology merely orders events, and indictment or diatribe would criticize rather than honor.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":8,"initial":"e"},{"id":382,"targetId":382,"type":"tc","text":"Calling the layoffs a “workforce adjustment” was a {{1}}, replacing a painful reality with softer language; its apparent neutrality {{2}} the human cost.","choices":[["euphemism","hyperbole","oxymoron"],["obscured","magnified","quantified"]],"answers":[["euphemism"],["obscured"]],"explanation":"A softened expression for something unpleasant is a euphemism. The bland wording hides, or obscures, the human impact rather than measuring or exaggerating it.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"obscured","meaning":"made harder to see or understand"}],"section":8,"initial":"e"},{"id":383,"targetId":383,"type":"se","text":"The normally reserved crowd became {{1}} when the long-awaited rescue succeeded, erupting into embraces and joyful cheers.","choices":[["euphoric","ecstatic","apprehensive","despondent","listless","resentful"]],"answers":[["euphoric","ecstatic"]],"explanation":"Intense shared happiness fits euphoric and ecstatic. The other choices describe anxiety, sadness, low energy, or bitterness.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"ecstatic","meaning":"intensely joyful"}],"section":8,"initial":"e"},{"id":384,"targetId":384,"type":"tc","text":"The contractor’s response was designed to {{1}} responsibility, shifting every question about the failure to a subcontractor who could not be reached.","choices":[["evade","assume","apportion","demonstrate","clarify"]],"answers":[["evade"]],"explanation":"Redirecting all questions away from oneself avoids responsibility, or evades it. Apportion would distribute responsibility rather than transfer every question as an escape tactic.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":8,"initial":"e"},{"id":385,"targetId":385,"type":"tc","text":"The colors were {{1}}, radiating light for only a moment before fading; the painter sought a more durable way to preserve their {{2}} quality.","choices":[["evanescent","indelible","permanent"],["luminous","opaque","drab"]],"answers":[["evanescent"],["luminous"]],"explanation":"Evanescent means quickly vanishing. The desired quality is radiance, or luminosity, which contrasts with opaque or drab colors.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"luminous","meaning":"radiant or full of light"}],"section":8,"initial":"e"},{"id":386,"targetId":386,"type":"tc","text":"The director’s replies remained {{1}}, addressing peripheral details while carefully avoiding the one issue on which the committee sought a firm commitment.","choices":[["evasive","forthright","categorical","exhaustive","unequivocal"]],"answers":[["evasive"]],"explanation":"Avoidance of the central question is evasive. Detail about peripheral matters does not make the answer complete or unambiguous.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":8,"initial":"e"},{"id":387,"targetId":387,"type":"tc","text":"A fragment of the old melody could {{1}} childhood memories with striking force, but their {{2}} did not guarantee accuracy; the historian cautioned against treating vivid recollection as {{3}} evidence.","choices":[["evoke","obliterate","chronicle"],["intensity","absence","incoherence"],["incontrovertible","fallible","dubious"]],"answers":[["evoke"],["intensity"],["incontrovertible"]],"explanation":"Evoke means bring to mind. Intense memories can still be mistaken, so their force does not make them incontrovertible, or impossible to dispute.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"intensity","meaning":"great strength or force"}],"section":8,"initial":"e"},{"id":388,"targetId":388,"type":"se","text":"The hurried repair would {{1}} the problem, enlarging the crack that it was supposed to seal.","choices":[["exacerbate","aggravate","ameliorate","resolve","diagnose","isolate"]],"answers":[["exacerbate","aggravate"]],"explanation":"Making the crack larger worsens the problem, so exacerbate and aggravate fit. The remaining choices improve, identify, or separate it rather than worsen it.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"aggravate","meaning":"make worse or more severe"}],"section":8,"initial":"e"},{"id":389,"targetId":389,"type":"tc","text":"The editor was {{1}}, rejecting any citation that could not be verified; this insistence on {{2}} sometimes slowed publication but made corrections rare.","choices":[["exacting","permissive","indolent"],["accuracy","novelty","brevity"]],"answers":[["exacting"],["accuracy"]],"explanation":"Exacting means demanding great care. Verifiable citations serve accuracy; novelty and brevity do not explain the checking requirement or rarity of corrections.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"accuracy","meaning":"correctness and freedom from error"}],"section":8,"initial":"e"},{"id":390,"targetId":390,"type":"tc","text":"The official history sought to {{1}} the ruler, attributing every success to his wisdom while omitting the mistakes that might diminish his reputation.","choices":[["exalt","depose","censure","humanize","lampoon"]],"answers":[["exalt"]],"explanation":"Selective praise elevates, or exalts, the ruler. Humanize would allow a more balanced portrait; censure and lampoon criticize or mock.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":8,"initial":"e"},{"id":391,"targetId":391,"type":"tc","text":"After receiving the same incomplete form for the fifth time, the clerk sounded {{1}}, her earlier patience finally exhausted.","choices":[["exasperated","placated","elated","indifferent","serene"]],"answers":[["exasperated"]],"explanation":"Repeated frustration exhausts patience and produces exasperation. The alternatives imply calm, satisfaction, happiness, or lack of concern.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":8,"initial":"e"},{"id":392,"targetId":392,"type":"tc","text":"The reviewer chose to {{1}} the book rather than offer measured criticism, and the ferocity of the attack brought him {{2}} rather than scholarly esteem.","choices":[["excoriate","commend","summarize"],["notoriety","anonymity","obscurity"]],"answers":[["excoriate"],["notoriety"]],"explanation":"Excoriate means criticize harshly. Public attention for a negative reason is notoriety; the passage contrasts that attention with earned scholarly respect.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"notoriety","meaning":"fame for an unfavorable reason"}],"section":8,"initial":"e"},{"id":393,"targetId":393,"type":"se","text":"The timestamped recording could {{1}} the technician, proving that she had left the building before the sabotage occurred.","choices":[["exculpate","absolve","implicate","incriminate","reprimand","indict"]],"answers":[["exculpate","absolve"]],"explanation":"Evidence clearing someone of responsibility can exculpate or absolve them. The alternatives blame, accuse, or rebuke rather than clear wrongdoing.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"absolve","meaning":"free from blame or guilt"}],"section":8,"initial":"e"},{"id":394,"targetId":394,"type":"tc","text":"The review was {{1}}: it examined every surviving draft, every relevant letter, and every published response before reaching its conclusion.","choices":[["exhaustive","cursory","selective","perfunctory","preliminary"]],"answers":[["exhaustive"]],"explanation":"Coverage of all relevant materials is exhaustive. The alternatives imply superficiality, selection, routine carelessness, or an unfinished stage.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":8,"initial":"e"},{"id":395,"targetId":395,"type":"tc","text":"For the young researcher, the discovery was {{1}}, replacing months of lethargy with renewed energy; progress thereafter continued {{2}}, with the next phase finished well ahead of schedule.","choices":[["exhilarating","enervating","dispiriting"],["apace","intermittently","imperceptibly"]],"answers":[["exhilarating"],["apace"]],"explanation":"Exhilarating means energizing and exciting. Apace means swiftly and fits the renewed momentum, whereas intermittently and imperceptibly do not express that rapid advance.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"apace","meaning":"at a swift rate"}],"section":8,"initial":"e"},{"id":396,"targetId":396,"type":"tc","text":"The final paragraph did not merely recommend reform; it sought to {{1}} readers to act immediately, warning that delay would make the losses irreversible.","choices":[["exhort","dissuade","permit","absolve","restrain"]],"answers":[["exhort"]],"explanation":"Exhort means urge strongly. The urgent call to act goes beyond permission and opposes dissuasion or restraint.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":8,"initial":"e"},{"id":397,"targetId":397,"type":"tc","text":"The flood created {{1}} demands on the council, making delay impossible; even usually {{2}} officials acted with unexpected {{3}}.","choices":[["exigent","trivial","deferred"],["dilatory","expeditious","prompt"],["celerity","torpor","hesitation"]],"answers":[["exigent"],["dilatory"],["celerity"]],"explanation":"Exigent means urgent. Officials normally given to delay are dilatory, and their unusually rapid response displays celerity, or speed.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"celerity","meaning":"swiftness of action"}],"section":8,"initial":"e"},{"id":398,"targetId":398,"type":"se","text":"The independent inquiry would {{1}} the researcher only if the records established that she had neither falsified the data nor authorized anyone else to do so.","choices":[["exonerate","vindicate","discredit","censure","implicate","reproach"]],"answers":[["exonerate","vindicate"]],"explanation":"Proof of no wrongdoing clears her: exonerate and vindicate fit. The other verbs blame, accuse, or damage her standing.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"vindicate","meaning":"clear of blame or suspicion"}],"section":8,"initial":"e"},{"id":399,"targetId":399,"type":"tc","text":"The publisher’s {{1}} fees seemed designed to exclude small institutions, which lacked the resources to absorb such {{2}}.","choices":[["exorbitant","nominal","modest"],["expenditure","revenue","frugality"]],"answers":[["exorbitant"],["expenditure"]],"explanation":"Exorbitant means unreasonably high. Fees are expenditure for the paying institution, not revenue or economical behavior.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"expenditure","meaning":"money or resources spent"}],"section":8,"initial":"e"},{"id":400,"targetId":400,"type":"tc","text":"The novelist described writing as a way to {{1}} the humiliating memory: by confronting it directly, she hoped to drive its lingering power from her life.","choices":[["exorcise","perpetuate","embellish","corroborate","evoke"]],"answers":[["exorcise"]],"explanation":"The metaphor concerns expelling a troubling influence. Exorcise fits; perpetuate preserves it and evoke brings it to mind without necessarily removing its power.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":8,"initial":"e"}]);
